1
00:00:01,252 --> 00:00:23,231
[SPEAKER_01]: So, last we talked, we talked about feelings, letting them rise on their own, and, I guess, for a time they persist, and then they go, not leaning towards the pressure of wanting more of it, or wanting less of whatever feeling has arisen.

2
00:00:28,186 --> 00:00:52,893
[SPEAKER_01]: And I've been reflecting on that for some time, and I'm trying to stop my old tendency of like feeling-feeling, or how am I feeling, and sort of like... those old mindfulness tendencies of sort of reflexively labeling and repeating to myself what's going on.

3
00:00:56,940 --> 00:00:59,979
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess, what came to my mind was, there's a determination...

4
00:01:05,687 --> 00:01:12,169
[SPEAKER_01]: To want more of it, if it is pleasing, or to not want it, if it's not pleasing.

5
00:01:13,130 --> 00:01:23,373
[SPEAKER_01]: And the determination, if it's a neutral feeling, is, I guess, most of the time, is sort of like... it persists for a time.

6
00:01:23,393 --> 00:01:28,355
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, oh, all right, this is... this is a neutral mood or attitude.

7
00:01:29,055 --> 00:01:40,623
[SPEAKER_01]: But then there's sort of like anxiety that builds up, and a restlessness, and then wanting to seek some kind of experience or stimulus.

8
00:01:41,844 --> 00:01:52,351
[SPEAKER_01]: I was thinking to myself, in line with reading a sutta involving the Buddha talking about thinking and pondering, or jhānas,

9
00:01:53,330 --> 00:02:05,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Even Brahma-viharas, these abodes of thinking and thought, in that way, it's sort of seeking a pleasant abiding, as it's said in the suttas.

10
00:02:06,379 --> 00:02:16,826
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha says, it's a blameless thing to seek out and have it, but at the same time, that is a desire to want.

11
00:02:17,767 --> 00:02:18,808
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, no, that's the thing.

12
00:02:20,927 --> 00:02:23,367
[SPEAKER_02]: Those pleasant abidings is not something you can want.

13
00:02:24,448 --> 00:02:31,909
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, in order to get the pleasant abiding of jhāna, first you need to stop running away from unpleasant abidings that are not jhāna.

14
00:02:33,169 --> 00:02:37,650
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, you can't use jhāna to replace the unpleasant abidings that bother you.

15
00:02:38,270 --> 00:02:39,991
[SPEAKER_02]: Restlessness, anxiety and so on.

16
00:02:40,331 --> 00:02:50,653
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why in every... whenever the Buddha describes the jhāna, he would say that worry and concern about the world have been abandoned beforehand,

17
00:02:51,752 --> 00:02:57,019
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't want jhāna.

18
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:06,110
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you can want it in a theoretical sense, like, oh, this is something worth developing it.

19
00:03:06,711 --> 00:03:10,236
[SPEAKER_02]: But developing jhāna cannot be rooted in your desire, which is why it's blameless.

20
00:03:12,108 --> 00:03:20,355
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when the Buddha talks about the pleasure of jhāna, it's the... basically, it's the pleasure of you not needing to run away from pain anymore.

21
00:03:20,735 --> 00:03:22,496
[SPEAKER_02]: That becomes pleasant because it's a relief.

22
00:03:23,597 --> 00:03:24,878
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're not bothered by the pain.

23
00:03:25,079 --> 00:03:26,240
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you enter jhāna.

24
00:03:26,958 --> 00:03:34,985
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't enter jhāna by replacing the feeling of discomfort or anxiety or whatever else with this great pleasure that is blameless.

25
00:03:35,446 --> 00:03:36,827
[SPEAKER_02]: That is sensuality, basically.

26
00:03:36,867 --> 00:03:38,068
[SPEAKER_02]: That's exactly how sensuality is.

27
00:03:38,949 --> 00:03:43,333
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why people turn to sensuality when they are, you know, a bit down or something bothers them.

28
00:03:44,234 --> 00:03:48,558
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, let me scratch my itch and experience pleasure and forget about things that bother me.

29
00:03:48,578 --> 00:03:49,439
[SPEAKER_02]: And yes, exactly.

30
00:03:49,479 --> 00:03:51,501
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why people meditate with their sensuality.

31
00:03:51,861 --> 00:03:52,602
[SPEAKER_02]: More often than not.

32
00:03:58,610 --> 00:04:09,739
[SPEAKER_01]: I wouldn't be able to achieve jhāna through, I guess, finding disagreeable feelings or unpleasant feelings, just trying to replace it.

33
00:04:10,380 --> 00:04:18,226
[SPEAKER_01]: And, like, in that sense, like, oh, right now I'm experiencing some sort of unpleasant mood or attitude.

34
00:04:18,607 --> 00:04:19,707
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let me

35
00:04:21,792 --> 00:04:30,639
[SPEAKER_01]: As I say, like, thinking and pondering upon a certain, as I say, metta or karuṇa... Yeah, you want to see why you're doing that.

36
00:04:30,859 --> 00:04:32,540
[SPEAKER_02]: See why you're trying to do that.

37
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:33,280
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

38
00:04:33,380 --> 00:04:37,763
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I'm thinking, pondering upon metta and karuṇa, because I want to get rid of this discomfort.

39
00:04:38,484 --> 00:04:40,726
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why you won't be getting rid of the discomfort correctly.

40
00:04:40,966 --> 00:04:42,046
[SPEAKER_02]: You're trying to replace it.

41
00:04:42,527 --> 00:04:49,652
[SPEAKER_02]: You're trying to do the practice, but still, doing the practice is secondary to you first wanting to be free from pain.

42
00:04:50,614 --> 00:04:55,175
[SPEAKER_02]: But the practice is basically not giving in on account of how you feel.

43
00:04:55,975 --> 00:04:57,415
[SPEAKER_02]: Not giving in to the actions of it.

44
00:04:58,216 --> 00:04:58,756
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean.

45
00:04:58,776 --> 00:05:01,716
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, practice becomes your action of dealing with pain.

46
00:05:02,676 --> 00:05:04,877
[SPEAKER_02]: And because of that, you won't be uprooting that pain.

47
00:05:09,118 --> 00:05:13,779
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when pain rises to just let it persist and... Well, let it persist.

48
00:05:14,299 --> 00:05:14,999
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, you will...

49
00:05:16,717 --> 00:05:18,658
[SPEAKER_02]: The thoughts will have to be there of some kind.

50
00:05:18,678 --> 00:05:28,380
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even if it's the thoughts that are hoping that the pain will disappear, as long as you don't lose perspective on those thoughts, you won't be giving in to that hope.

51
00:05:30,521 --> 00:05:32,601
[SPEAKER_02]: You will be practicing indifference regardless.

52
00:05:32,981 --> 00:05:38,903
[SPEAKER_02]: So, hoping that the pain disappear will be seen as part of the pain, not part of what you want and what you feel entitled to.

53
00:05:42,805 --> 00:06:01,972
[SPEAKER_01]: So, directing, I mean, the entirety... your mind is just directed only on feeling, and by, like, I guess, being attentive to that feeling arising, not trying to direct it elsewhere, or trying to direct it... No, again, see, why?

54
00:06:02,292 --> 00:06:04,533
[SPEAKER_02]: Why would that be a problem, if you direct it elsewhere?

55
00:06:05,013 --> 00:06:05,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that a problem?

56
00:06:07,114 --> 00:06:09,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I can see, sort of, like, I mean, thoughts,

57
00:06:10,542 --> 00:06:11,482
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, that's restlessness.

58
00:06:11,582 --> 00:06:14,003
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do you... or rather, why do you need to stay with the feeling?

59
00:06:16,084 --> 00:06:18,225
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do you must not allow your thoughts to go elsewhere?

60
00:06:19,726 --> 00:06:34,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then I'll become distracted by my thoughts and then I'll start thinking either something pointed towards sensuality, distracting myself through my thoughts with sensuality of what I could be getting and forgetting that I'm even feeling anything.

61
00:06:34,211 --> 00:06:37,933
[SPEAKER_02]: So... and then why would you not want to be distracted from sensuality?

62
00:06:39,717 --> 00:06:41,137
[SPEAKER_01]: Distracted by sensuality.

63
00:06:41,457 --> 00:06:41,637
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

64
00:06:41,657 --> 00:06:42,598
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah.

65
00:06:42,698 --> 00:06:43,878
[SPEAKER_02]: Why would you want to avoid that?

66
00:06:45,998 --> 00:06:50,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that would be me trying to find...

67
00:06:50,599 --> 00:06:54,080
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, that would be my normal tendency of finding an escape, right?

68
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:55,120
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure, sure.

69
00:06:55,280 --> 00:07:00,842
[SPEAKER_02]: But I'm not asking why would you... That's the reason why you would go to sensuality, to find escape.

70
00:07:01,782 --> 00:07:04,603
[SPEAKER_02]: So, why are you now not wanting to go to sensuality?

71
00:07:05,103 --> 00:07:06,343
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you don't want to find escape?

72
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:18,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I want to develop knowledge and understanding in regard to, I guess, dhamma and become free from that... From what?

73
00:07:20,108 --> 00:07:21,729
[SPEAKER_01]: The craving.

74
00:07:22,690 --> 00:07:30,736
[SPEAKER_01]: That sort of tendency or impulse to move away from... And why would you want to be free from that?

75
00:07:34,119 --> 00:07:38,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it leads to...

76
00:07:40,393 --> 00:07:46,058
[SPEAKER_01]: It leads to thinking in regards to unwholesome thoughts and to pain.

77
00:07:46,158 --> 00:07:50,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, it leads to a further pain down the line in the future.

78
00:07:50,642 --> 00:07:52,684
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it leads to further pain.

79
00:07:53,525 --> 00:07:54,866
[SPEAKER_02]: Does that mean it's already painful?

80
00:08:02,352 --> 00:08:06,095
[SPEAKER_01]: I suppose it would be because it's like an agitated...

81
00:08:06,796 --> 00:08:07,076
[SPEAKER_02]: It is.

82
00:08:08,318 --> 00:08:08,958
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why.

83
00:08:09,418 --> 00:08:11,399
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you want to not experience pain.

84
00:08:13,299 --> 00:08:18,461
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you would go to sensuality with previous habits, because you don't want to experience pain.

85
00:08:19,421 --> 00:08:23,882
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, you're restraining sensuality for the same reason, because you don't want to experience pain.

86
00:08:25,702 --> 00:08:27,563
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, you're affected by the pain.

87
00:08:29,156 --> 00:08:37,139
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to recognize then that... and that's why, see, that's why sensory strain cannot automatically result in freedom from pain.

88
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:43,262
[SPEAKER_02]: But, that's why sensory strain is a necessary basis for the result of being free from pain.

89
00:08:43,922 --> 00:08:48,885
[SPEAKER_02]: But that result will never occur for as long as your mind just stays on the level of what you're doing.

90
00:08:48,945 --> 00:08:51,766
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, I'm doing sensuality or I'm doing restraint of sensuality.

91
00:08:53,057 --> 00:09:01,203
[SPEAKER_02]: So, restraint from sensuality is necessary, but in itself, it's still the same action rooted in the same reason of trying to avoid pain.

92
00:09:03,164 --> 00:09:10,930
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why just restraining itself, you need to maintain, but if there is no wisdom, sensuality is still kind of the value, so to speak.

93
00:09:10,990 --> 00:09:17,435
[SPEAKER_02]: So, now you're just saying no to it, which is a necessary step, but in itself cannot lead you to the result that you want.

94
00:09:19,143 --> 00:09:25,633
[SPEAKER_02]: And the result that you want is when you start recognizing that, through sense restraint, being established beforehand, you start recognizing that,

95
00:09:27,051 --> 00:09:29,112
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter what you... even like this sense of duty.

96
00:09:29,132 --> 00:09:32,274
[SPEAKER_02]: I must practice dhamma for this, for this, for the future prevention of pain.

97
00:09:32,394 --> 00:09:41,479
[SPEAKER_02]: All of that is secondary to the fact that right now, right here, you have experience of discomfort against which you crave to not be.

98
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:45,061
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the root of all of your pains.

99
00:09:45,701 --> 00:09:47,002
[SPEAKER_02]: Future pains, past pains.

100
00:09:47,742 --> 00:09:49,183
[SPEAKER_02]: Not what actually happened to you.

101
00:09:49,664 --> 00:09:53,966
[SPEAKER_02]: So, see, even like sensuality is not a direct cause of pain.

102
00:09:55,401 --> 00:09:58,942
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's hard to uproot it, because you don't necessarily see the connection.

103
00:10:00,943 --> 00:10:03,284
[SPEAKER_02]: But sensuality is direct cause of craving.

104
00:10:04,085 --> 00:10:05,245
[SPEAKER_02]: Proliferation of craving.

105
00:10:06,305 --> 00:10:11,328
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, once you start equating craving and suffering, then you realize, yeah, sensuality is the cause of suffering.

106
00:10:11,488 --> 00:10:12,488
[SPEAKER_02]: Right here, right now.

107
00:10:12,528 --> 00:10:13,448
[SPEAKER_02]: Not even in the future.

108
00:10:14,269 --> 00:10:19,211
[SPEAKER_02]: As you said, you're already pressured and agitated by the pressure of that direction.

109
00:10:19,511 --> 00:10:20,631
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's already unpleasant.

110
00:10:21,672 --> 00:10:22,812
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means pain is already there.

111
00:10:22,852 --> 00:10:23,012
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

112
00:10:23,032 --> 00:10:24,453
[SPEAKER_02]: Because craving is already there.

113
00:10:26,690 --> 00:10:30,692
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't want to prevent pain from coming your way, because you can't.

114
00:10:30,712 --> 00:10:35,934
[SPEAKER_02]: But you can certainly prevent maintenance of craving for which you're responsible.

115
00:10:37,515 --> 00:10:41,316
[SPEAKER_02]: And you're responsible by giving in to pain, or giving in to pleasure.

116
00:10:42,257 --> 00:10:44,798
[SPEAKER_02]: By taking things up, you maintain the craving of it.

117
00:10:47,119 --> 00:10:49,880
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why it's always important to keep the context as to why.

118
00:10:51,578 --> 00:10:52,358
[SPEAKER_02]: Not all because of this.

119
00:10:52,679 --> 00:10:56,321
[SPEAKER_02]: See, you were explaining all because of future suffering, because of this, because of this.

120
00:10:56,741 --> 00:11:01,124
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you keep pushing it, it's always going to come back to here and now, because it cannot be anywhere else.

121
00:11:01,684 --> 00:11:06,006
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't experience suffering today on account of craving from yesterday.

122
00:11:07,968 --> 00:11:09,609
[SPEAKER_02]: You can experience craving today

123
00:11:10,504 --> 00:11:11,985
[SPEAKER_02]: on account of which you suffer today.

124
00:11:12,545 --> 00:11:16,547
[SPEAKER_02]: And yes, that craving has been maintained yesterday and day before yesterday and day before yesterday.

125
00:11:17,007 --> 00:11:20,088
[SPEAKER_02]: But your suffering is not ever because of previous cravings.

126
00:11:20,408 --> 00:11:21,789
[SPEAKER_02]: It's only because of the present craving.

127
00:11:22,269 --> 00:11:23,710
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's felt presently.

128
00:11:25,110 --> 00:11:30,132
[SPEAKER_02]: Or you cannot experience, you cannot feel the feeling of any kind in the past.

129
00:11:31,353 --> 00:11:33,934
[SPEAKER_02]: And you cannot feel the feeling of any kind in the future.

130
00:11:34,414 --> 00:11:36,175
[SPEAKER_02]: It can only be felt presently.

131
00:11:37,295 --> 00:11:40,277
[SPEAKER_02]: It can be on account of future things, sure, but it's felt presently.

132
00:11:41,238 --> 00:11:45,742
[SPEAKER_02]: It can be on account of past actions, past things that happened to you, but it's felt presently.

133
00:11:47,400 --> 00:11:47,920
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean.

134
00:11:48,361 --> 00:11:53,263
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot prevent the feeling from occurring, because it's not within your domain of control.

135
00:11:53,804 --> 00:11:54,764
[SPEAKER_02]: You are subjected to it.

136
00:11:55,325 --> 00:11:56,725
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, in the same sense, you don't need to...

137
00:11:56,766 --> 00:11:58,767
[SPEAKER_02]: I must stay mindful of this, in a sense of...

138
00:11:58,787 --> 00:12:03,129
[SPEAKER_02]: I must be just looking at this feeling, because, whether you look at it or not, it's felt there.

139
00:12:03,630 --> 00:12:07,852
[SPEAKER_02]: So, really, you need to learn how to feel it, without needing to stare at it all the time.

140
00:12:09,473 --> 00:12:10,474
[SPEAKER_01]: This... this...

141
00:12:11,780 --> 00:12:22,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Forgetfulness that occurs during, like, you know, I mean, being aware of it, and I'm like, oh, okay, so this is my mood, this is my attitude currently.

142
00:12:22,964 --> 00:12:29,707
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if anxiety or restlessness occurs, then the thoughts just, like, start... Yeah, and that's not the problem.

143
00:12:29,847 --> 00:12:31,008
[SPEAKER_02]: That's part of the anxiety.

144
00:12:31,628 --> 00:12:35,149
[SPEAKER_02]: It's you losing the sight of the whole picture on account of what's happening.

145
00:12:35,749 --> 00:12:37,550
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you lose your recollectedness.

146
00:12:37,590 --> 00:12:39,591
[SPEAKER_02]: Not because of what type of thoughts are there.

147
00:12:41,370 --> 00:12:46,653
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you have thoughts of restlessness and anxiety, you include it within the situation, part of the situation.

148
00:12:47,294 --> 00:12:47,494
Yeah.

149
00:12:48,214 --> 00:12:57,640
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, I guess, extremely, I mean, in that sense, just, it's like a tide, like, just a flood, just, like, sort of sweeps me out.

150
00:12:58,080 --> 00:13:00,942
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it's like, I don't even remember I'm in my kuti anymore.

151
00:13:01,202 --> 00:13:01,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

152
00:13:01,382 --> 00:13:02,983
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't even remember I'm here in Sri Lanka.

153
00:13:03,124 --> 00:13:03,424
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

154
00:13:04,357 --> 00:13:08,640
[SPEAKER_02]: See, even that is not necessarily a problem.

155
00:13:08,900 --> 00:13:11,842
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't need to constantly tell yourself that you're in the Kuti or you're in Sri Lanka.

156
00:13:12,423 --> 00:13:14,724
[SPEAKER_02]: Your situation, then, is those thoughts.

157
00:13:15,625 --> 00:13:16,766
[SPEAKER_02]: But you include that.

158
00:13:17,651 --> 00:13:19,492
[SPEAKER_02]: That's secondary to the feeling.

159
00:13:19,512 --> 00:13:20,072
[SPEAKER_02]: Not something...

160
00:13:20,092 --> 00:13:20,412
[SPEAKER_02]: I must...

161
00:13:20,432 --> 00:13:21,952
[SPEAKER_02]: I must prevent this, I must remove this.

162
00:13:22,973 --> 00:13:25,353
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it's just part of the experience that's currently enduring.

163
00:13:25,373 --> 00:13:26,193
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the whole point.

164
00:13:26,634 --> 00:13:31,415
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing that experience comes and goes on its own, and you are subjected to it, it's not other way around.

165
00:13:31,955 --> 00:13:34,416
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means you can't choose what you're subjected to it.

166
00:13:36,476 --> 00:13:37,457
[SPEAKER_02]: Certainly not directly.

167
00:13:37,517 --> 00:13:41,458
[SPEAKER_02]: You can choose to not be subjected to sensuality by leading the life of restraint.

168
00:13:42,859 --> 00:13:44,741
[SPEAKER_02]: By avoiding the central environment and so on.

169
00:13:44,801 --> 00:13:49,685
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's only like kind of circumstantial level of control, which is all you can do.

170
00:13:49,785 --> 00:13:52,547
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you have to develop these things beforehand, as the Buddha would say.

171
00:13:53,268 --> 00:13:58,192
[SPEAKER_02]: But yes, so that's why, like, in Satipaṭṭhāna, he knows the mind affected with lust is mind affected with lust.

172
00:13:58,593 --> 00:14:01,475
[SPEAKER_02]: Mind affected with restlessness is mind affected with restlessness.

173
00:14:01,555 --> 00:14:04,638
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, it's not you must deal with this, you must prevent it, you must tackle it.

174
00:14:05,038 --> 00:14:05,158
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

175
00:14:05,318 --> 00:14:07,080
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the mind affected by this thinking like this.

176
00:14:07,520 --> 00:14:08,701
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't lose perspective on it.

177
00:14:08,721 --> 00:14:09,642
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the extent of your

178
00:14:11,162 --> 00:14:13,344
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like this must stop occurring.

179
00:14:14,325 --> 00:14:17,568
[SPEAKER_02]: That is you overshooting the mark, which is why you remain affected by it.

180
00:14:18,209 --> 00:14:29,600
[SPEAKER_01]: So, during these moments of restlessness and constant thinking, when I sort of... Don't try to get rid of it and don't give in to it.

181
00:14:30,481 --> 00:14:31,081
[SPEAKER_02]: How you do that?

182
00:14:31,101 --> 00:14:33,443
[SPEAKER_02]: You do that by... when you want to get rid of it, you don't.

183
00:14:34,224 --> 00:14:35,906
[SPEAKER_02]: Or when you want to give in, you don't.

184
00:14:37,993 --> 00:14:44,558
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't want to tell you, oh, do this or do that, because then that will become, like, your output for trying to get rid of it.

185
00:14:45,119 --> 00:14:57,729
[SPEAKER_02]: See, if I say, well, perhaps you could see what is your body doing while you're restless, you might see that accurately first, but then, looking at what my body is doing while I'm restless will be what you do in order to get rid of the restlessness.

186
00:15:00,031 --> 00:15:00,812
[SPEAKER_02]: And you don't want that.

187
00:15:02,093 --> 00:15:05,435
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yes, in a way, include it in your situation.

188
00:15:05,455 --> 00:15:07,177
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, your body still needs to be there.

189
00:15:08,188 --> 00:15:13,590
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you're completely overwhelmed by the thoughts in your mind, it's still on the account of the body presently enduring in a seated position.

190
00:15:14,990 --> 00:15:18,831
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just the... it's the pressure of the tendency of wanting to get rid of the pain.

191
00:15:19,652 --> 00:15:20,512
[SPEAKER_02]: That's always the problem.

192
00:15:20,572 --> 00:15:22,292
[SPEAKER_02]: That's always why you will lose perspective.

193
00:15:22,933 --> 00:15:25,173
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you need to start training in regard to that.

194
00:15:26,634 --> 00:15:33,696
[SPEAKER_02]: Not giving in to the pressure to get rid of the pain, which means you need to learn how to endure it on that right level.

195
00:15:34,648 --> 00:15:36,588
[SPEAKER_02]: And sometimes you lose the sight, sometimes you won't.

196
00:15:37,309 --> 00:15:48,011
[SPEAKER_02]: If the precepts are established beforehand, you won't act out of it in an extremely unwholesome manner, then everything else can be contained, and then you can start seeing through that eventually.

197
00:15:48,131 --> 00:15:48,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Is, eh...

198
00:15:49,412 --> 00:15:57,314
[SPEAKER_01]: I suppose, like, recollecting, like, after these states, like, you know, have passed, like, sometimes they just... they lose momentum on their own.

199
00:15:57,454 --> 00:16:02,415
[SPEAKER_02]: I won't... Yeah, you need to recollect that you mustn't try to get rid of it.

200
00:16:04,430 --> 00:16:08,814
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, that's basically the root of your recollection, because that's the root of your motivation.

201
00:16:09,895 --> 00:16:17,841
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, what exactly you recollect, through the recollection of, I mustn't get rid of this, that's secondary.

202
00:16:18,201 --> 00:16:19,863
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you can have any reference point.

203
00:16:20,243 --> 00:16:21,524
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't need to be a specific one.

204
00:16:22,505 --> 00:16:29,871
[SPEAKER_02]: But if I tell you, oh, recollect the body, you will be unaware that the motivation behind you recollecting your body is trying to get rid of the pain.

205
00:16:30,752 --> 00:16:34,053
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is the same motivation as why you would give to sensuality in the past.

206
00:16:34,833 --> 00:16:37,934
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is the motivation that perpetuates the craving in regard to how you feel.

207
00:16:38,614 --> 00:16:39,735
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why it's not freeing you.

208
00:16:40,515 --> 00:16:45,236
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why psychology and psychotherapy is impotent when it comes to actually uprooting the problem.

209
00:16:45,476 --> 00:16:46,417
[SPEAKER_02]: You can only manage it.

210
00:16:48,017 --> 00:16:53,499
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to recognize that now this is unpleasant means I want to get rid of it.

211
00:16:53,559 --> 00:16:57,380
[SPEAKER_02]: Recognize that context, cultivate that context, and that's how you stop trying to get rid of it.

212
00:16:59,365 --> 00:17:01,768
[SPEAKER_02]: When you stop trying to get rid of it, you stop craving against it.

213
00:17:02,349 --> 00:17:04,112
[SPEAKER_02]: Absence of craving means absence of suffering.

214
00:17:04,472 --> 00:17:07,937
[SPEAKER_02]: That's when you get to understand that anxiety was not unpleasant.

215
00:17:08,558 --> 00:17:09,519
[SPEAKER_02]: Things were not unpleasant.

216
00:17:09,579 --> 00:17:12,203
[SPEAKER_02]: It was your own craving in regard to the things that was unpleasant.

217
00:17:14,981 --> 00:17:34,268
[SPEAKER_01]: For, I guess, most of my life, I mean, since I've read the suttas and since I began monastic life and living at a monastery, I took it on myself to read a sutta, the removal of distracting thoughts, and I had always sort of followed that as a method.

218
00:17:34,908 --> 00:17:40,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, if this thought is going to lead down, you know, into the unwholesome, then it's better to...

219
00:17:41,091 --> 00:17:44,252
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, they had the four different methods.

220
00:17:46,700 --> 00:17:48,241
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize it for what it is.

221
00:17:48,261 --> 00:17:48,481
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

222
00:17:48,721 --> 00:17:51,543
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it will go on its own or substitute it.

223
00:17:51,563 --> 00:17:52,404
[SPEAKER_01]: Try the opposite.

224
00:17:52,424 --> 00:17:53,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Crush it, mind in mind.

225
00:17:54,005 --> 00:17:54,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

226
00:17:54,605 --> 00:17:54,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

227
00:17:54,805 --> 00:17:55,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to stop it.

228
00:17:56,246 --> 00:18:05,192
[SPEAKER_01]: And I guess, I still have that same sort of tendency of being like, okay, so this, you know, is bad and I must, you know, substitute it.

229
00:18:05,212 --> 00:18:05,892
[SPEAKER_02]: I must get rid of it.

230
00:18:06,052 --> 00:18:06,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

231
00:18:06,373 --> 00:18:06,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

232
00:18:06,653 --> 00:18:06,813
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

233
00:18:07,633 --> 00:18:11,076
[SPEAKER_02]: No, but see, even that sutta, substituting the thoughts, means first understanding what they are.

234
00:18:11,896 --> 00:18:19,042
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the problem when people read that sutta or generally, when he doesn't tolerate thoughts of ill-will or thoughts of sensuality, means, I must get rid, no?

235
00:18:20,424 --> 00:18:22,706
[SPEAKER_02]: You tolerating it or not, is on you.

236
00:18:23,226 --> 00:18:25,068
[SPEAKER_02]: Arising of the thing is not on you.

237
00:18:26,209 --> 00:18:29,872
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, when you read, not tolerate, you think, I must get rid of its presence.

238
00:18:30,853 --> 00:18:33,755
[SPEAKER_02]: There's a simile I was giving before, like somebody comes at your doorstep.

239
00:18:36,138 --> 00:18:37,739
[SPEAKER_02]: You let them in or you don't.

240
00:18:38,059 --> 00:18:38,640
[SPEAKER_02]: That's on you.

241
00:18:40,785 --> 00:18:43,987
[SPEAKER_02]: If you close the door, they can stand as long as they want out there.

242
00:18:44,807 --> 00:18:45,548
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not a problem.

243
00:18:46,308 --> 00:18:49,010
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why we say he doesn't welcome the thoughts of sensuality.

244
00:18:49,431 --> 00:18:50,531
[SPEAKER_02]: He doesn't delight in it.

245
00:18:50,872 --> 00:18:52,012
[SPEAKER_02]: He doesn't entertain it.

246
00:18:53,974 --> 00:18:55,114
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you get rid of it.

247
00:18:56,115 --> 00:19:00,578
[SPEAKER_02]: The person will leave your doorstep when he realizes he's not coming in.

248
00:19:01,399 --> 00:19:03,420
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, he will not be allowed in.

249
00:19:03,940 --> 00:19:04,401
[SPEAKER_02]: He gives up.

250
00:19:05,341 --> 00:19:06,282
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's saying the matter.

251
00:19:07,522 --> 00:19:12,763
[SPEAKER_02]: He still assails and attacks, but like the enlightened mind, he realizes, oh, I'm wasting my time here.

252
00:19:13,023 --> 00:19:14,003
[SPEAKER_02]: And then the Māra leaves.

253
00:19:14,383 --> 00:19:17,224
[SPEAKER_02]: Then you stop having even those assailing thoughts.

254
00:19:18,804 --> 00:19:22,365
[SPEAKER_02]: But not understanding that, like, to what extent is your responsibility?

255
00:19:23,125 --> 00:19:25,206
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you think, I must go out and chase it.

256
00:19:25,666 --> 00:19:29,487
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way you get involved, either way that person sees that he has effect on you.

257
00:19:30,467 --> 00:19:32,627
[SPEAKER_02]: Whether you let them in or shout them, you acknowledge them.

258
00:19:33,808 --> 00:19:35,248
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the point.

259
00:19:35,268 --> 00:19:35,888
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all they want.

260
00:19:36,495 --> 00:19:38,976
[SPEAKER_02]: Mahārāja wants acknowledgement, recognition.

261
00:19:44,719 --> 00:19:47,100
[SPEAKER_01]: So, recollecting the context.

262
00:19:47,801 --> 00:19:52,123
[SPEAKER_02]: Recollecting the... like being aware of the content in order to recollect the context.

263
00:19:53,564 --> 00:19:54,884
[SPEAKER_02]: Not trying to get rid of the content.

264
00:19:55,705 --> 00:19:58,506
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the context you want to cultivate.

265
00:19:58,666 --> 00:20:02,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing the content and recollecting the context.

266
00:20:02,978 --> 00:20:07,901
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, knowing in terms of just seeing what's there, not in terms of analyzing or figuring it out.

267
00:20:07,981 --> 00:20:08,722
[SPEAKER_01]: That's irrelevant.

268
00:20:08,842 --> 00:20:11,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of just pushing it away, so it's not even in my view.

269
00:20:11,464 --> 00:20:11,844
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

270
00:20:11,864 --> 00:20:13,325
[SPEAKER_02]: Not trying to deny the content.

271
00:20:13,565 --> 00:20:14,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

272
00:20:14,506 --> 00:20:14,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

273
00:20:31,878 --> 00:20:32,418
[SPEAKER_01]: And...

274
00:20:34,365 --> 00:20:46,411
[SPEAKER_01]: That my own... that seeing of the content, recollecting of the context, that increases my understanding.

275
00:20:47,471 --> 00:20:52,473
[SPEAKER_02]: And so... Well, it increases familiarity now with that relationship.

276
00:20:53,494 --> 00:20:55,715
[SPEAKER_02]: And then everything else that you know from suttas can be

277
00:20:56,462 --> 00:21:00,603
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you can begin seeing it against that new familiarity.

278
00:21:00,923 --> 00:21:01,043
[SPEAKER_02]: Ah!

279
00:21:01,523 --> 00:21:04,444
[SPEAKER_02]: See, I used to read that she doesn't tolerate the central thought.

280
00:21:04,784 --> 00:21:07,524
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, that can mean I go and try and get rid of it.

281
00:21:08,624 --> 00:21:12,085
[SPEAKER_02]: But it can also mean I don't tolerate it for whatever it has arisen.

282
00:21:13,526 --> 00:21:14,406
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't entertain it.

283
00:21:14,526 --> 00:21:15,706
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't say, yes, come, please.

284
00:21:16,106 --> 00:21:16,226
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

285
00:21:16,986 --> 00:21:17,886
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I'm not welcoming it.

286
00:21:17,946 --> 00:21:18,847
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not entertaining it.

287
00:21:18,907 --> 00:21:20,787
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not delighted on account of it.

288
00:21:22,180 --> 00:21:31,222
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then you realize the things you thought you understood can be understood in even... in a different manner, in a greater manner, so to speak, in a more accurate manner.

289
00:21:31,903 --> 00:21:35,544
[SPEAKER_02]: Because now you're developing the new context you're becoming familiar with.

290
00:21:35,584 --> 00:21:41,665
[SPEAKER_02]: Content and context of the content.

291
00:21:41,725 --> 00:21:44,006
[SPEAKER_02]: Content is what you can attend to directly.

292
00:21:45,686 --> 00:21:47,207
[SPEAKER_02]: Context is the peripheral of it.

293
00:21:50,408 --> 00:21:51,188
[SPEAKER_01]: That's whatever is...

294
00:21:54,112 --> 00:21:55,773
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

295
00:21:55,933 --> 00:21:57,454
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, there's this, there's this.

296
00:21:57,714 --> 00:21:59,935
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, the context of this is, I don't want it.

297
00:21:59,975 --> 00:22:00,835
[SPEAKER_02]: I want to get rid of it.

298
00:22:00,915 --> 00:22:01,815
[SPEAKER_02]: I want to act out of it.

299
00:22:01,956 --> 00:22:02,996
[SPEAKER_02]: I want it to disappear.

300
00:22:03,916 --> 00:22:05,097
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the context of your action.

301
00:22:05,117 --> 00:22:10,959
[SPEAKER_02]: But then you realize that context is contradictory, because I even didn't have seen this arising, yet here I am hoping I can remove it.

302
00:22:11,680 --> 00:22:12,740
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why you suffer.

303
00:22:14,501 --> 00:22:16,382
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't suffer because of the arising of the thing.

304
00:22:17,582 --> 00:22:19,263
[SPEAKER_02]: You suffer because you're craving towards it.

305
00:22:22,494 --> 00:22:32,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking of my previous learning and understanding is, you know, there's like gross levels of suffering that one does away with.

306
00:22:32,241 --> 00:22:36,924
[SPEAKER_01]: One doesn't participate in the worldly life because they see, oh, like... Yeah.

307
00:22:37,344 --> 00:22:41,847
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that is, that is, that is true on that kind of circumstantial level.

308
00:22:42,788 --> 00:22:50,473
[SPEAKER_02]: But then there's the more subtle kinds of... Yeah, like you don't become a sotāpanna by gradually removing your puthujjana suffering.

309
00:22:51,735 --> 00:22:55,218
[SPEAKER_02]: You become a sotāpanna, like what the suttas say, by uprooting the thorn of suffering.

310
00:22:56,619 --> 00:23:00,902
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, as a result of that, all of your other suffering will fade.

311
00:23:01,823 --> 00:23:05,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you remove the root, the source of it.

312
00:23:07,047 --> 00:23:10,450
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the important thing to understand.

313
00:23:11,110 --> 00:23:18,516
[SPEAKER_02]: Because otherwise you think that all these little circumstantial practices you do, are somehow magically contributing to this suffering diminishing.

314
00:23:21,073 --> 00:23:21,833
[SPEAKER_02]: It's other way around.

315
00:23:22,074 --> 00:23:23,654
[SPEAKER_02]: First, you need to understand what suffering is.

316
00:23:24,135 --> 00:23:26,956
[SPEAKER_02]: And by doing so, you fully uproot the core of it.

317
00:23:27,516 --> 00:23:29,417
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, all the other residues will fade away.

318
00:23:30,598 --> 00:23:33,779
[SPEAKER_02]: But not like through fading away of the residues, I will deplete the core.

319
00:23:34,079 --> 00:23:34,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Nope.

320
00:23:37,081 --> 00:23:40,202
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why, again, the four overtruths is what makes a sotāpanna.

321
00:23:41,103 --> 00:23:43,424
[SPEAKER_02]: There is suffering, as in suffering is there.

322
00:23:43,484 --> 00:23:44,004
[SPEAKER_02]: Understand it.

323
00:23:44,785 --> 00:23:45,685
[SPEAKER_02]: Understand what it is.

324
00:23:46,205 --> 00:23:47,786
[SPEAKER_02]: Understand what causes it.

325
00:23:47,946 --> 00:23:49,607
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the reason for suffering?

326
00:23:51,473 --> 00:23:54,377
[SPEAKER_02]: So, which means, until the Sotāpatti, you don't even know what suffering is.

327
00:23:54,978 --> 00:23:55,939
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's exactly the case.

328
00:23:56,440 --> 00:24:00,545
[SPEAKER_02]: Putujana thinks suffering is in the eyes, in the sights, in the sounds, smells, tastes, touches, and so on.

329
00:24:01,566 --> 00:24:04,010
[SPEAKER_02]: Suffering is in what such and such said to you.

330
00:24:04,891 --> 00:24:06,253
[SPEAKER_02]: Suffering is in not getting this.

331
00:24:08,635 --> 00:24:13,739
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you secondarily remove your desires, so you don't want things in the future, so you don't get disappointed, so you don't suffer.

332
00:24:14,179 --> 00:24:16,240
[SPEAKER_02]: That is, as I said, circumstantially correct.

333
00:24:16,300 --> 00:24:21,104
[SPEAKER_02]: And yeah, it's still better than being heedless completely, but you're not uprooting the suffering.

334
00:24:21,604 --> 00:24:24,867
[SPEAKER_02]: Suffering was never in getting what you want or not getting what you want.

335
00:24:25,587 --> 00:24:29,570
[SPEAKER_02]: Suffering was always rooted in presence of craving in regard to the presence of feeling.

336
00:24:32,332 --> 00:24:33,753
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what you need to understand.

337
00:24:33,773 --> 00:24:36,475
[SPEAKER_02]: If you understand that, all the other suffering will be depleted.

338
00:24:38,878 --> 00:24:41,519
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes sooner, sometimes later, but it will have to be depleted.

339
00:24:47,001 --> 00:24:49,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Understand craving in regards to feeling.

340
00:24:49,602 --> 00:24:49,802
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

341
00:24:51,063 --> 00:24:53,144
[SPEAKER_02]: So, first, you need to recognize what feeling is.

342
00:24:55,185 --> 00:24:56,365
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, stop trying to get rid of it.

343
00:24:56,925 --> 00:24:57,986
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop trying to go towards it.

344
00:24:58,266 --> 00:24:59,867
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop trying to go away from it.

345
00:24:59,887 --> 00:25:00,747
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop trying to ignore it.

346
00:25:02,528 --> 00:25:07,890
[SPEAKER_02]: By practicing that, you're practicing attitudes that are not compatible with craving.

347
00:25:09,152 --> 00:25:12,335
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how craving diminishes.

348
00:25:12,375 --> 00:25:16,159
[SPEAKER_01]: I think about, like, eating a meal.

349
00:25:16,859 --> 00:25:19,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, I mean, we get varying kinds of food.

350
00:25:19,422 --> 00:25:28,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes some food is better than... depending on what day it is, the quality, or, you know, the sugar content, or along those lines.

351
00:25:29,891 --> 00:25:30,232
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

352
00:25:31,642 --> 00:25:45,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding, as I'm eating, the content of the food, and this is sweet, this is, you know, tasty, you know, this is gonna give me, make me, I guess, give me more pleasant feelings of eating or something along those lines.

353
00:25:45,532 --> 00:25:47,573
[SPEAKER_01]: But understand that, that context.

354
00:25:47,593 --> 00:25:49,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I mean, not rejecting the food.

355
00:25:49,955 --> 00:25:50,475
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you can't.

356
00:25:50,636 --> 00:25:51,796
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly, exactly.

357
00:25:52,137 --> 00:25:55,239
[SPEAKER_02]: And same, you know, you can try and avoid the taste.

358
00:25:57,140 --> 00:25:57,881
[SPEAKER_02]: And always

359
00:26:00,309 --> 00:26:02,951
[SPEAKER_02]: Eat what you don't want to eat.

360
00:26:03,011 --> 00:26:06,353
[SPEAKER_02]: Thinking that's how sometime... somehow you will avoid the craving.

361
00:26:06,433 --> 00:26:06,853
[SPEAKER_02]: But you won't.

362
00:26:06,893 --> 00:26:08,314
[SPEAKER_02]: You're already acting out of that craving.

363
00:26:09,235 --> 00:26:13,758
[SPEAKER_02]: You just recognize that you cannot remain indifferent towards taste, so you're trying to hide it.

364
00:26:15,579 --> 00:26:19,601
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, you need to develop a greater context than the context of taste.

365
00:26:21,002 --> 00:26:25,305
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to develop a greater reason for eating than the reason of taste.

366
00:26:27,677 --> 00:26:29,158
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, just keep mind on that level.

367
00:26:29,178 --> 00:26:31,478
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter how much you enjoy it or not, the food.

368
00:26:32,459 --> 00:26:33,879
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is the greater context?

369
00:26:33,939 --> 00:26:41,822
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, as the Buddha said, you reflect, you ponder, you keep in your mind, even when you don't eat, the reason why you would eat.

370
00:26:41,862 --> 00:26:47,904
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just to stay healthy, to have enough strength to endure elements, and so you can practice the dhamma.

371
00:26:49,112 --> 00:26:52,954
[SPEAKER_02]: And you eat only to the point of diminishing the hunger, not overeating.

372
00:26:53,634 --> 00:26:55,394
[SPEAKER_02]: So, keep that context while you're eating.

373
00:26:55,735 --> 00:26:57,735
[SPEAKER_02]: Enjoyment or non-enjoyment becomes secondary.

374
00:26:58,596 --> 00:27:06,879
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's the most agreeable food you can have, but you are only keeping your mind on, am I still full or am I not full?

375
00:27:08,300 --> 00:27:08,900
[SPEAKER_02]: You feel full?

376
00:27:08,980 --> 00:27:09,220
[SPEAKER_02]: Fine.

377
00:27:09,480 --> 00:27:09,940
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop eating.

378
00:27:10,620 --> 00:27:11,061
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter.

379
00:27:11,621 --> 00:27:12,781
[SPEAKER_00]: What else is left to be eaten?

380
00:27:14,902 --> 00:27:15,702
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't feel full?

381
00:27:15,902 --> 00:27:16,663
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop worrying.

382
00:27:17,577 --> 00:27:20,699
[SPEAKER_02]: About how tasty the food is, because that's not the reason you eat it.

383
00:27:21,140 --> 00:27:27,565
[SPEAKER_02]: Once you start worrying about if... what... oh, am I... am I... start doubting it, you lose... lost the context of the right reasons for eating.

384
00:27:28,125 --> 00:27:33,909
[SPEAKER_02]: The hindrance of doubt, hindrance of worry, hindered you, obstructed you, and now you...

385
00:27:34,390 --> 00:27:38,193
[SPEAKER_02]: So, see, by worrying about the taste, you give power to the taste.

386
00:27:39,254 --> 00:27:41,655
[SPEAKER_02]: Trying to get rid of the taste, you give power to the taste.

387
00:27:42,176 --> 00:27:44,998
[SPEAKER_02]: By indulging the taste carelessly, you give power to the taste.

388
00:27:47,352 --> 00:27:54,334
[SPEAKER_02]: By not worrying about any of those movements, but maintaining the context of mindfulness, the reason for eating is removing hunger.

389
00:27:55,775 --> 00:27:57,816
[SPEAKER_02]: The moment hunger is gone, you stop eating.

390
00:27:57,856 --> 00:27:58,196
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

391
00:27:58,336 --> 00:27:59,836
[SPEAKER_02]: You remain true to the right principle.

392
00:28:00,177 --> 00:28:01,797
[SPEAKER_02]: Your mindfulness remains undiminished.

393
00:28:02,217 --> 00:28:04,258
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if it doesn't feel right.

394
00:28:05,779 --> 00:28:06,819
[SPEAKER_02]: That doesn't matter how you feel.

395
00:28:08,279 --> 00:28:11,060
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not supposed to mean, oh, it's supposed to feel right.

396
00:28:11,160 --> 00:28:15,282
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not always going to feel pleasantly, because, yeah, when the hindrance is present, it's going to torment you.

397
00:28:17,001 --> 00:28:17,661
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's the point.

398
00:28:17,961 --> 00:28:20,842
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not acting anymore from how you feel.

399
00:28:21,763 --> 00:28:23,383
[SPEAKER_02]: You're acting from the right context.

400
00:28:23,983 --> 00:28:26,284
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even as that hindrance is enduring...

401
00:28:26,304 --> 00:28:26,744
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

402
00:28:27,685 --> 00:28:28,425
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the point.

403
00:28:28,505 --> 00:28:29,765
[SPEAKER_02]: Hindrances don't disappear.

404
00:28:31,206 --> 00:28:31,926
[SPEAKER_02]: Even in jhāna.

405
00:28:33,027 --> 00:28:35,127
[SPEAKER_02]: You disappear from the domain of hindrances.

406
00:28:36,788 --> 00:28:38,849
[SPEAKER_02]: But they remain living where they were.

407
00:28:39,669 --> 00:28:41,169
[SPEAKER_02]: Because all hindrances are bodily.

408
00:28:41,690 --> 00:28:42,610
[SPEAKER_02]: They're bound with the body.

409
00:28:44,271 --> 00:28:47,233
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why, you know, they're felt as all this discomfort and everything else.

410
00:28:48,033 --> 00:28:51,135
[SPEAKER_02]: Even doubt and restlessness.

411
00:28:51,155 --> 00:28:53,836
[SPEAKER_02]: Your mind is bound with the body, in the same sense.

412
00:28:56,498 --> 00:28:59,539
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't overcome hindrances by preventing them from arising.

413
00:28:59,579 --> 00:29:03,181
[SPEAKER_02]: You overcome hindrances by being imperturbable regardless of their arising or not.

414
00:29:04,462 --> 00:29:06,303
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you are not hindered by them.

415
00:29:08,706 --> 00:29:12,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Kind of imperturbableness comes from... Not acting out of feeling.

416
00:29:13,049 --> 00:29:14,589
[SPEAKER_02]: And always understand the context.

417
00:29:15,710 --> 00:29:17,751
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like always, I must always understand it.

418
00:29:17,771 --> 00:29:18,612
[SPEAKER_02]: Simply not losing it.

419
00:29:18,872 --> 00:29:19,492
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, not losing it.

420
00:29:19,512 --> 00:29:19,712
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

421
00:29:20,453 --> 00:29:24,475
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, understanding of the context doesn't require attentive maintenance.

422
00:29:25,396 --> 00:29:27,077
[SPEAKER_02]: Just requires clarity of understanding.

423
00:29:27,177 --> 00:29:27,537
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

424
00:29:27,877 --> 00:29:28,798
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it becomes effortless.

425
00:29:29,563 --> 00:29:35,747
[SPEAKER_01]: That reminds me, like in a sutra, where someone asked the Buddha, like, an arahant, like, how does he know...

426
00:29:35,787 --> 00:29:38,429
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, does he... is he always thinking about his knowledge?

427
00:29:38,489 --> 00:29:41,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Is he the only one who reviews his knowledge?

428
00:29:41,291 --> 00:29:42,192
[SPEAKER_02]: Or something prompts it?

429
00:29:43,613 --> 00:29:49,377
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not obsessing, like, constantly... And that's also how arahants still have preferences.

430
00:29:50,177 --> 00:29:51,578
[SPEAKER_02]: I prefer to do this over this.

431
00:29:51,838 --> 00:29:53,820
[SPEAKER_02]: This is more agreeable, this is less agreeable.

432
00:29:54,420 --> 00:29:56,642
[SPEAKER_01]: None of that is rooted in desire and craving anymore.

433
00:29:59,466 --> 00:30:08,568
[SPEAKER_02]: But, if you refuse to ever look beyond the preferences, beyond the pleasure that you get through eating, you refuse to look at any different context.

434
00:30:10,548 --> 00:30:12,749
[SPEAKER_02]: And sometimes people refuse to look that way because they're afraid.

435
00:30:13,609 --> 00:30:21,331
[SPEAKER_02]: They're afraid that if I look away from, I must avoid pleasurable tastes, then I will automatically go back to pleasurable tastes.

436
00:30:22,291 --> 00:30:23,031
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's not the case.

437
00:30:23,651 --> 00:30:26,252
[SPEAKER_02]: You go back to pleasurable tastes when you choose to go back to pleasurable tastes.

438
00:30:27,090 --> 00:30:32,333
[SPEAKER_02]: But you remain bound with the dependence on pleasurable taste for as long as you're trying to deny the pleasurability of the taste.

439
00:30:33,914 --> 00:30:38,917
[SPEAKER_02]: So, they think that through denying, they can replace the dependence on pleasurable taste.

440
00:30:39,637 --> 00:30:40,518
[SPEAKER_02]: And because of that, they don't.

441
00:30:41,578 --> 00:30:48,963
[SPEAKER_02]: So, only through discerning the greater context, regardless of pleasurable or displeasurable taste, you get to surmount the dependence on the taste.

442
00:30:49,343 --> 00:30:56,287
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you understand, like, the gratification of the taste, the danger,

443
00:30:57,266 --> 00:31:00,148
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's like... those are not four different things you would understand.

444
00:31:00,788 --> 00:31:03,470
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like one thing that gets fully understood in all these facets.

445
00:31:03,770 --> 00:31:04,891
[SPEAKER_02]: In the context of suffering.

446
00:31:05,011 --> 00:31:08,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

447
00:31:08,253 --> 00:31:16,718
[SPEAKER_01]: And sometimes, I mean, in the suttas, sometimes the Buddha breaks up in like mahadukkha khandhā, knowing the gratification, danger, and then he knows the escape.

448
00:31:16,738 --> 00:31:16,898
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

449
00:31:17,018 --> 00:31:20,760
[SPEAKER_02]: Whenever he uses that kind of formula to describe, means full understanding.

450
00:31:21,961 --> 00:31:25,183
[SPEAKER_02]: Like full, complete clarity in regard to that context.

451
00:31:27,303 --> 00:31:30,606
[SPEAKER_01]: But no need or method to recollect the danger.

452
00:31:31,507 --> 00:31:37,573
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you'll be... you won't be... See, you will need to recollect the danger, because you haven't fully understood it.

453
00:31:37,833 --> 00:31:39,295
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means you haven't fully understood the danger.

454
00:31:39,815 --> 00:31:42,297
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, you should be trying to understand the danger, of course.

455
00:31:43,018 --> 00:31:46,281
[SPEAKER_02]: But don't think that that's what the Buddha means by this formula.

456
00:31:46,321 --> 00:31:47,302
[SPEAKER_02]: He fully understands it.

457
00:31:48,748 --> 00:31:53,815
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you need to fully understand your dependence on it, your freedom from it.

458
00:31:54,576 --> 00:31:58,861
[SPEAKER_02]: When you're fully free, means you're fully understood the danger, the escape, the gratification and so on.

